IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ccc-core`

-- ccc17-seizure.l4 -- Criminal Code (Canada), R.S.C., 1985, c. C-46, sections 489 to 490.01: seizure of things not specified, seizure without a warrant, the report to a justice, detention of
-- things seized, their return and disposal, appeals, and perishable things. Layer 1: the law, objectively. Same conventions as ccc17-dna-orders.l4.
-- Periods: "three clear days notice" is asked as a number of clear days (the sources do not define a "clear day"); the 180 days, one year and 30 days are computed from dates. A "may" gated by
-- "where", "when" or "after" answers JUST FALSE when the gate fails (fork group G1, for a lawyer); a "may" gated by "if" answers NOTHING (fork F1).

§ `Section 489 -- Seizure of things not specified; seizure without warrant`

-- covers: 489(1), 489(1)(a), 489(1)(b), 489(1)(c)
-- jural: a person executing a warrant > the person from whom the thing is seized > power
-- src:9956 | 489 (1) Every person who executes a warrant may seize, in addition to the things mentioned in the warrant, any thing that the person believes on reasonable grounds
-- src:9957 | (a) has been obtained by the commission of an offence against this or any other Act of Parliament;
-- src:9958 | (b) has been used in the commission of an offence against this or any other Act of Parliament; or
-- src:9959 | (c) will afford evidence in respect of an offence against this or any other Act of Parliament.
@ref s 489(1) (src:9956-9959)
GIVEN `the person executes a warrant` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person believes on reasonable grounds that the thing has been obtained by the commission of an offence against this or any other Act of Parliament` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person believes on reasonable grounds that the thing has been used in the commission of an offence against this or any other Act of Parliament` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person believes on reasonable grounds that the thing will afford evidence in respect of an offence against this or any other Act of Parliament` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 489(1) the person who executes a warrant may seize the thing in addition to the things mentioned in the warrant` MEANS
    IF `the person executes a warrant` AND (`the person believes on reasonable grounds that the thing has been obtained by the commission of an offence against this or any other Act of Parliament` OR `the person believes on reasonable grounds that the thing has been used in the commission of an offence against this or any other Act of Parliament` OR `the person believes on reasonable grounds that the thing will afford evidence in respect of an offence against this or any other Act of Parliament`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording)

-- covers: 489(2), 489(2)(a), 489(2)(b), 489(2)(c)
-- jural: a peace officer or a public officer > the person from whom the thing is seized > power
-- src:9961 | (2) Every peace officer, and every public officer who has been appointed or designated to administer or enforce any federal or provincial law and whose duties include the enforcement of this or any other Act of Parliament, who is lawfully present in a place pursuant to a warrant or otherwise in the execution of duties may, without a warrant, seize any thing that the officer believes on reasonable grounds
-- src:9962 | (a) has been obtained by the commission of an offence against this or any other Act of Parliament;
-- src:9963 | (b) has been used in the commission of an offence against this or any other Act of Parliament; or
-- src:9964 | (c) will afford evidence in respect of an offence against this or any other Act of Parliament.
@ref s 489(2) (src:9961-9964)
GIVEN `s 2 peace officer: the person is one` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the person is a public officer who has been appointed or designated to administer or enforce any federal or provincial law and whose duties include the enforcement of this or any other Act of Parliament` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is lawfully present in a place pursuant to a warrant or otherwise in the execution of duties` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the officer believes on reasonable grounds that the thing has been obtained by the commission of an offence against this or any other Act of Parliament` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the officer believes on reasonable grounds that the thing has been used in the commission of an offence against this or any other Act of Parliament` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the officer believes on reasonable grounds that the thing will afford evidence in respect of an offence against this or any other Act of Parliament` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 489(2) the officer may, without a warrant, seize the thing` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`s 2 peace officer: the person is one` EQUALS JUST TRUE OR `the person is a public officer who has been appointed or designated to administer or enforce any federal or provincial law and whose duties include the enforcement of this or any other Act of Parliament`) THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording)
        IF NOT `the person is lawfully present in a place pursuant to a warrant or otherwise in the execution of duties` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording)
        IF `the officer believes on reasonable grounds that the thing has been obtained by the commission of an offence against this or any other Act of Parliament` OR `the officer believes on reasonable grounds that the thing has been used in the commission of an offence against this or any other Act of Parliament` OR `the officer believes on reasonable grounds that the thing will afford evidence in respect of an offence against this or any other Act of Parliament` THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording)

§ `Section 489.1 -- Restitution of thing or report`

DECLARE `s 489.1(1) what the peace officer shall do` IS ONE OF
    `s 489.1(1)(a) return the thing seized, on being issued a receipt for it, to the person lawfully entitled to its possession and report to a justice having jurisdiction in respect of the matter`
    `s 489.1(1)(b) bring the thing seized before a justice, or report to the justice that the thing has been seized and is being detained, to be dealt with in accordance with subsection 490(1)`

-- covers: 489.1(1), 489.1(1)(a), 489.1(1)(b)
-- jural: the person lawfully entitled to possession > a peace officer > right
-- src:9966 | 489.1 (1) Subject to this or any other Act of Parliament, if a peace officer has seized anything under a warrant issued under this Act, under section 487.11 or 489, or otherwise in the execution of duties under this or any other Act of Parliament, the peace officer shall, as soon as is practicable,
-- src:9967 | (a) return the thing seized, on being issued a receipt for it, to the person lawfully entitled to its possession and report to a justice having jurisdiction in respect of the matter and, in the case of a warrant, jurisdiction in the province in which the warrant was issued, if the peace officer is satisfied that
-- src:9968 | (i) there is no dispute as to who is lawfully entitled to possession of the thing seized, and
-- src:9969 | (ii) the continued detention of the thing seized is not required for the purposes of any investigation or a preliminary inquiry, trial or other proceeding; or
-- src:9970 | (b) bring the thing seized before a justice referred to in paragraph (a), or report to the justice that the thing has been seized and is being detained, to be dealt with in accordance with subsection 490(1), if the peace officer is not satisfied as described in subparagraphs (a)(i) and (ii).
@ref s 489.1(1) (src:9966-9970)
-- "as soon as is practicable": undefined period, no deadline invented. Paragraph (a) when the officer is satisfied of both (i) and (ii); paragraph (b) when not so satisfied.
GIVEN `a peace officer has seized anything under a warrant issued under this Act, under section 487.11 or 489, or otherwise in the execution of duties under this or any other Act of Parliament` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the peace officer is satisfied that there is no dispute as to who is lawfully entitled to possession of the thing seized` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the peace officer is satisfied that the continued detention of the thing seized is not required for the purposes of any investigation or a preliminary inquiry, trial or other proceeding` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE `s 489.1(1) what the peace officer shall do`
`s 489.1(1) what the peace officer shall do as soon as is practicable` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `a peace officer has seized anything under a warrant issued under this Act, under section 487.11 or 489, or otherwise in the execution of duties under this or any other Act of Parliament` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording)
        IF `the peace officer is satisfied that there is no dispute as to who is lawfully entitled to possession of the thing seized` AND `the peace officer is satisfied that the continued detention of the thing seized is not required for the purposes of any investigation or a preliminary inquiry, trial or other proceeding` THEN JUST `s 489.1(1)(a) return the thing seized, on being issued a receipt for it, to the person lawfully entitled to its possession and report to a justice having jurisdiction in respect of the matter`
        OTHERWISE JUST `s 489.1(1)(b) bring the thing seized before a justice, or report to the justice that the thing has been seized and is being detained, to be dealt with in accordance with subsection 490(1)`

-- covers: 489.1(2), 489.1(2)(a), 489.1(2)(b)
-- jural: the person lawfully entitled to possession > a person other than a peace officer who has seized the thing > right
-- src:9972 | (2) Subject to this or any other Act of Parliament, if a person other than a peace officer has seized anything under a warrant issued under this Act, under section 487.11 or 489, or otherwise in the execution of duties under this or any other Act of Parliament, that person shall, as soon as is practicable and so that the thing seized may be dealt with in accordance with subsection 490(1),
-- src:9973 | (a) bring the thing before a justice having jurisdiction in respect of the matter and, in the case of a warrant, jurisdiction in the province in which the warrant was issued; or
-- src:9974 | (b) report to the justice referred to in paragraph (a) that the thing has been seized and is being detained.
@ref s 489.1(2) (src:9972-9974)
GIVEN `a person other than a peace officer has seized anything under a warrant issued under this Act, under section 487.11 or 489, or otherwise in the execution of duties under this or any other Act of Parliament` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 489.1(2) the person shall, as soon as is practicable, bring the thing before a justice or report to the justice that it has been seized and is being detained` MEANS
    IF `a person other than a peace officer has seized anything under a warrant issued under this Act, under section 487.11 or 489, or otherwise in the execution of duties under this or any other Act of Parliament`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording)

-- covers: 489.1(3)
-- jural: none
-- src:9976 | (3) A report to a justice under this section shall be in Form 5.2, varied to suit the case.
@ref s 489.1(3) (src:9976)
GIVEN `the report is made to a justice under section 489.1` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 489.1(3) the report to a justice shall be in Form 5.2, varied to suit the case` MEANS
    IF `the report is made to a justice under section 489.1`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording)

-- covers: 489.1(4)
-- jural: none
-- src:9978 | (4) This section does not apply with respect to computer data, as defined in subsection 342.1(2), other than virtual currency or other digital assets.
@ref s 489.1(4) (src:9978)
-- "This section does not apply with respect to computer data ..., other than virtual currency or other digital assets": computer data other than those: JUST TRUE; those: the text's own no (JUST FALSE).
GIVEN `the thing is computer data as defined in subsection 342.1(2)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the thing is virtual currency or other digital assets` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 489.1(4) section 489.1 does not apply to the thing` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the thing is computer data as defined in subsection 342.1(2)` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording)
        IF `the thing is virtual currency or other digital assets` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "other than virtual currency or other digital assets"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

§ `Section 490 -- Detention of things seized`

DECLARE `s 490 forum` IS ONE OF
    `s 490 a judge of a superior court of criminal jurisdiction or a judge as defined in section 552`
    `s 490 a justice`

DECLARE `s 490(1) order` IS ONE OF
    `s 490(1)(a) order the thing seized to be returned to the lawful owner or the person lawfully entitled to possession`
    `s 490(1)(b) detain the thing seized or order that it be detained, taking reasonable care to ensure that it is preserved`

-- covers: 490(1), 490(1)(a), 490(1)(b)
-- jural: the lawful owner or person lawfully entitled to possession > a justice > right
-- src:9980 | 490 (1) Subject to this or any other Act of Parliament, where, pursuant to paragraph 489.1(1)(b) or subsection 489.1(2), anything that has been seized is brought before a justice or a report in respect of anything seized is made to a justice, the justice shall,
-- src:9981 | (a) where the lawful owner or person who is lawfully entitled to possession of the thing seized is known, order it to be returned to that owner or person, unless the prosecutor, or the peace officer or other person having custody of the thing seized, satisfies the justice that the detention of the thing seized is required for the purposes of any investigation or a preliminary inquiry, trial or other proceeding; or
-- src:9982 | (b) where the prosecutor, or the peace officer or other person having custody of the thing seized, satisfies the justice that the thing seized should be detained for a reason set out in paragraph (a), detain the thing seized or order that it be detained, taking reasonable care to ensure that it is preserved until the conclusion of any investigation or until it is required to be produced for the purposes of a preliminary inquiry, trial or other proceeding.
@ref s 490(1) (src:9980-9982)
GIVEN `the thing seized is brought before a justice, or a report in respect of it is made to a justice, pursuant to paragraph 489.1(1)(b) or subsection 489.1(2)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the lawful owner or person who is lawfully entitled to possession of the thing seized is known` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the prosecutor, or the peace officer or other person having custody of the thing seized, satisfies the justice that the detention of the thing seized is required for the purposes of any investigation or a preliminary inquiry, trial or other proceeding` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE `s 490(1) order`
`s 490(1) the order the justice shall make` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the thing seized is brought before a justice, or a report in respect of it is made to a justice, pursuant to paragraph 489.1(1)(b) or subsection 489.1(2)` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording)
        IF `the prosecutor, or the peace officer or other person having custody of the thing seized, satisfies the justice that the detention of the thing seized is required for the purposes of any investigation or a preliminary inquiry, trial or other proceeding` THEN JUST `s 490(1)(b) detain the thing seized or order that it be detained, taking reasonable care to ensure that it is preserved`
        IF `the lawful owner or person who is lawfully entitled to possession of the thing seized is known` THEN JUST `s 490(1)(a) order the thing seized to be returned to the lawful owner or the person lawfully entitled to possession`
        OTHERWISE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (silent)

-- covers: 490(2), 490(2)(a), 490(2)(b)
-- jural: the person from whom the thing was seized > a peace officer or the prosecutor > right
-- src:9984 | (2) Nothing shall be detained under the authority of paragraph (1)(b) for a period of more than 180 days after the day of the seizure, or any longer period that ends when an application made under paragraph (a) is decided, unless
-- src:9985 | (a) a justice, on the making of a summary application to him after three clear days notice thereof to the person from whom the thing detained was seized, is satisfied that, having regard to the nature of the investigation, its further detention for a specified period is warranted and the justice so orders; or
-- src:9986 | (b) proceedings are instituted in which the thing detained may be required.
@ref s 490(2) (src:9984-9986)
-- A bar on detaining "for a period of more than 180 days after the day of the seizure, or any longer period that ends when an application made under paragraph (a) is decided, unless (a) ... or (b) ...".
-- JUST TRUE: the bar applies; JUST FALSE: the "unless" is met; NOTHING: the day is within the period the bar speaks of (limited by its own wording).
GIVEN `the day of the seizure` IS A DATE
      `the day in question` IS A DATE
      `an application under paragraph (a) has been made and is not yet decided` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a justice has ordered further detention for a specified period under paragraph (a) and that period has not ended` IS A BOOLEAN
      `proceedings are instituted in which the thing detained may be required` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 490(2) the section bars detaining the thing for more than 180 days after the day of the seizure` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the day in question` AT MOST `s 490(2) the last day on which the thing may be detained without a further order` `the day of the seizure` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording)
        IF `an application under paragraph (a) has been made and is not yet decided` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording)
        IF `a justice has ordered further detention for a specified period under paragraph (a) and that period has not ended` OR `proceedings are instituted in which the thing detained may be required` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless (a) a justice, on the making of a summary application to him after three clear days notice thereof to the person from whom the thing detained was seized"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

@ref s 490(2) (the 180 days) (src:9984)
GIVEN `the day of the seizure` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 490(2) the last day on which the thing may be detained without a further order` MEANS
    `the day of the seizure` PLUS 180

-- covers: 490(3), 490(3)(a), 490(3)(b)
-- jural: the person from whom the thing was seized > a peace officer or the prosecutor > right
-- src:9988 | (3) More than one order for further detention may be made under paragraph (2)(a) but the cumulative period of detention shall not exceed one year from the day of the seizure, or any longer period that ends when an application made under paragraph (a) is decided, unless
-- src:9989 | (a) a judge of a superior court of criminal jurisdiction or a judge as defined in section 552, on the making of a summary application to him after three clear days notice thereof to the person from whom the thing detained was seized, is satisfied, having regard to the complex nature of the investigation, that the further detention of the thing seized is warranted for a specified period and subject to such other conditions as the judge considers just, and the judge so orders; or
-- src:9990 | (b) proceedings are instituted in which the thing detained may be required.
@ref s 490(3) (src:9988-9990)
-- Same shape as subsection (2), for the cumulative period of one year from the day of the seizure; the extension is by a judge of a superior court of criminal jurisdiction or a judge as defined in section 552.
GIVEN `the day of the seizure` IS A DATE
      `the day in question` IS A DATE
      `an application under paragraph (a) has been made and is not yet decided` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a judge has ordered further detention for a specified period under paragraph (a) and that period has not ended` IS A BOOLEAN
      `proceedings are instituted in which the thing detained may be required` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 490(3) the section bars detaining the thing for more than one year from the day of the seizure` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the day in question` AT MOST `s 490(3) the last day on which the thing may be detained without a judge’s order` `the day of the seizure` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording)
        IF `an application under paragraph (a) has been made and is not yet decided` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording)
        IF `a judge has ordered further detention for a specified period under paragraph (a) and that period has not ended` OR `proceedings are instituted in which the thing detained may be required` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless (a) a judge of a superior court of criminal jurisdiction or a judge as defined in section 552"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- One year from the day of the seizure (fork F5: the text has no rule for counting the day; a seizure on 29 February is carried to 28 February by `add years`).
-- fork: F5
@ref s 490(3) (the one year) (src:9988)
GIVEN `the day of the seizure` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 490(3) the last day on which the thing may be detained without a judge’s order` MEANS
    `add years` `the day of the seizure` 1

-- covers: 490(3.1)
-- fork: F1
-- jural: the lawful owner > a peace officer or the prosecutor > power
-- src:9992 | (3.1) A thing may be detained under paragraph (1)(b) for any period, whether or not an application for an order under subsection (2) or (3) is made, if the lawful owner or person who is lawfully entitled to possession of the thing seized consents in writing to its detention for that period.
@ref s 490(3.1) (src:9992)
GIVEN `the lawful owner or person who is lawfully entitled to possession of the thing seized consents in writing to its detention for that period` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 490(3.1) the thing may be detained for the period` MEANS
    IF `the lawful owner or person who is lawfully entitled to possession of the thing seized consents in writing to its detention for that period`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording)

-- covers: 490(4)
-- jural: the clerk of the court > a justice > right
-- src:9994 | (4) When an accused has been ordered to stand trial, the justice shall forward anything detained pursuant to subsections (1) to (3) to the clerk of the court to which the accused has been ordered to stand trial to be detained by the clerk of the court and disposed of as the court directs.
@ref s 490(4) (src:9994)
GIVEN `an accused has been ordered to stand trial` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 490(4) the justice shall forward anything detained to the clerk of the court to which the accused has been ordered to stand trial` MEANS
    IF `an accused has been ordered to stand trial`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "When an accused has been ordered to stand trial"

-- The court that hears the applications of subsections (5) to (11), (15) and (17): a judge of a superior court of criminal jurisdiction or a judge as defined in section 552 where a judge ordered
-- the detention under subsection (3); a justice in any other case. Total over the one fact.
-- covers: 490(5), 490(5)(a), 490(5)(b), 490(5) closing words
-- jural: the person from whom the thing was seized or who claims to be its owner > the prosecutor, peace officer or other person having custody > right
-- src:9996 | (5) Where at any time before the expiration of the periods of detention provided for or ordered under subsections (1) to (3) in respect of anything seized, the prosecutor, or the peace officer or other person having custody of the thing seized, determines that the continued detention of the thing seized is no longer required for any purpose mentioned in subsection (1) or (4), the prosecutor, peace officer or other person shall apply to
-- src:9997 | (a) a judge of a superior court of criminal jurisdiction or a judge as defined in section 552, where a judge ordered its detention under subsection (3), or
-- src:9998 | (b) a justice, in any other case,
-- src:9999 | who shall, after affording the person from whom the thing was seized or the person who claims to be the lawful owner thereof or person entitled to its possession, if known, an opportunity to establish that he is lawfully entitled to the possession thereof, make an order in respect of the property under subsection (9).
@ref s 490(5) (src:9996-9999)
GIVEN `a judge ordered the detention of the thing seized under subsection (3)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `s 490 forum`
`s 490(5) the court to which the application is made` MEANS
    IF `a judge ordered the detention of the thing seized under subsection (3)`
    THEN `s 490 a judge of a superior court of criminal jurisdiction or a judge as defined in section 552`
    ELSE `s 490 a justice`

@ref s 490(5) (the duty to apply) (src:9996-9999)
GIVEN `before the expiration of the periods of detention provided for or ordered under subsections (1) to (3) the prosecutor, or the peace officer or other person having custody of the thing seized, determines that the continued detention of the thing seized is no longer required for any purpose mentioned in subsection (1) or (4)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 490(5) the prosecutor, peace officer or other person shall apply for an order in respect of the property under subsection (9)` MEANS
    IF `before the expiration of the periods of detention provided for or ordered under subsections (1) to (3) the prosecutor, or the peace officer or other person having custody of the thing seized, determines that the continued detention of the thing seized is no longer required for any purpose mentioned in subsection (1) or (4)`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where at any time before the expiration of the periods of detention provided for or ordered under subsections (1) to (3) in respect of anything seized, the prosecutor, or the peace officer or other person having custody of the thing seized, determines that the continued detention of the thing seized is no longer required"

-- covers: 490(6)
-- jural: the person from whom the thing was seized > the prosecutor, peace officer or other person having custody > right
-- src:10001 | (6) Where the periods of detention provided for or ordered under subsections (1) to (3) in respect of anything seized have expired and proceedings have not been instituted in which the thing detained may be required, the prosecutor, peace officer or other person shall apply to a judge or justice referred to in paragraph (5)(a) or (b) in the circumstances set out in that paragraph, for an order in respect of the property under subsection (9) or (9.1).
@ref s 490(6) (src:10001)
GIVEN `the periods of detention provided for or ordered under subsections (1) to (3) have expired` IS A BOOLEAN
      `proceedings have not been instituted in which the thing detained may be required` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 490(6) the prosecutor, peace officer or other person shall apply for an order in respect of the property under subsection (9) or (9.1)` MEANS
    IF `the periods of detention provided for or ordered under subsections (1) to (3) have expired` AND `proceedings have not been instituted in which the thing detained may be required`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where the periods of detention provided for or ordered under subsections (1) to (3) in respect of anything seized have expired and proceedings have not been instituted in which the thing detained may be required"

-- covers: 490(7), 490(7)(a), 490(7)(b)
-- fork: G1
-- jural: the person from whom anything was seized > a judge or justice > power
-- src:10003 | (7) A person from whom anything has been seized may, after the expiration of the periods of detention provided for or ordered under subsections (1) to (3) and on three clear days notice to the Attorney General, apply summarily to
-- src:10004 | (a) a judge of a superior court of criminal jurisdiction or a judge as defined in section 552, where a judge ordered the detention of the thing seized under subsection (3), or
-- src:10005 | (b) a justice, in any other case,
-- src:10006 | for an order under paragraph (9)(c) that the thing seized be returned to the applicant.
@ref s 490(7) (src:10003-10006)
-- "may, after the expiration of the periods ... and on three clear days notice to the Attorney General, apply summarily": a "may" gated by "after" and by a notice period; the periods ruling (b) and (c) answer JUST FALSE
-- when the gate fails (fork group G1, for a lawyer). Early application is allowed by subsection (8) and the notice by subsection (18).
GIVEN `the periods of detention provided for or ordered under subsections (1) to (3) have expired` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the court has allowed the application to be made before the expiration of the periods under subsection (8)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the clear days of notice given to the Attorney General` IS A NUMBER
      `the Attorney General has agreed under subsection (18) that the application be made before the expiration of the three days` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 490(7) the person from whom anything has been seized may apply summarily for an order under paragraph (9)(c) that the thing be returned` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the periods of detention provided for or ordered under subsections (1) to (3) have expired` OR `the court has allowed the application to be made before the expiration of the periods under subsection (8)`) THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "may, after the expiration of the periods of detention provided for or ordered under subsections (1) to (3)"
        IF `the clear days of notice given to the Attorney General` LESS THAN 3 AND NOT `the Attorney General has agreed under subsection (18) that the application be made before the expiration of the three days` THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "on three clear days notice to the Attorney General"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- covers: 490(8)
-- fork: G1
-- jural: the person from whom anything was seized > a judge or justice > power
-- src:10008 | (8) A judge of a superior court of criminal jurisdiction or a judge as defined in section 552, where a judge ordered the detention of the thing seized under subsection (3), or a justice, in any other case, may allow an application to be made under subsection (7) prior to the expiration of the periods referred to therein where he is satisfied that hardship will result unless the application is so allowed.
